popen as POSIX and glibc have it (after patch 0037). The child of a
popen kept every stream an earlier popen had opened, which POSIX has it
close: a reader of a command's output then waited forever, its writer
held open in a later child. The mode was searched for "w" anywhere, so
"rw" wrote and "rx" read; glibc takes 'r' or 'w' and any 'e'
(FD_CLOEXEC), in any order, and fails with EINVAL otherwise. When the
pipe's child end already had the number it moves to (stdin or stdout
closed in the caller), the child closed it after the no-op dup2. A
failed allocation panicked, and SIGINT and SIGQUIT were ignored in the
caller and SIGCHLD blocked while the child started, as system() does but
popen does not. Now the pipe is close-on-exec from the start, so that a
program another thread starts holds neither end, a lock-protected list
of open popen streams is closed in each child, the child's end keeps its
number when it has it, and an allocation failure is ENOMEM. Meant for
upstream.

